# Pricing under a sandbox

The pricing math in this Sales workbook touches money: a tax rule that
quotes a final figure, a currency conversion that reaches the network, and
the lead [[score]] those numbers ride on. We want all of it grouped, capped,
and accountable — without rewriting any of the units. That grouping is what
a **sandbox** gives us.

A **sandbox is a named isolation unit, not a placement.** Placement (the
thing covered in the previous chapter) answers *where code runs* — `client`
or `server`, and only those two. A sandbox answers a different question:
*what is this code allowed to touch, and how much may it spend?* A unit still
has a placement; the sandbox wraps a capability boundary around it. You can
have a server unit inside a sandbox and a client unit inside a different one;
`sandbox` never decides where the code runs.

## Grouping units by nesting

The simplest way to put units in a sandbox is to nest them inside a
`sandbox :name do … end` block. Everything declared in the block joins that
boundary. Here `:pricing` groups a new `:tax` unit with the lead [[score]],
so the units that decide a quote share one wall. `:tax` is defined right in
the block; `:score` lives in its own file and joins by name (next section).

sandbox :pricing do
  server compute :tax do
    @rate_by_region %{us: 0.0, eu: 0.20, uk: 0.20, ca: 0.13}

    def quote(amount, region) do
      rate = Map.get(@rate_by_region, region, 0.0)
      tax = round(amount * rate)
      %{net: amount, tax: tax, gross: amount + tax, region: region}
    end
  end
end

`:tax` is a `server compute` unit — native Elixir on the BEAM (the nexus
tier), trusted, no wasm. Nesting it in `:pricing` did not change that. It
declared that this unit shares an isolation unit: a single named boundary the
weave can audit, meter, and reason about as one.

## Joining from elsewhere with `in:`

Nesting is convenient when you author a unit and its sandbox together, but a
unit defined in another file can join a sandbox by name with `in:`. That is
how the [[score]] unit — which lives in its own file because the dashboard
and the [[pipeline]] both reference it directly — actually lands in
`:pricing` without being physically rewritten under the block above:

server compute :score, in: :pricing do
  # the same score/1 defined in [[score]] — this is membership, not a
  # redefinition; the body and the formula stay where score is authored
end

`in: :pricing` and nesting inside `sandbox :pricing do … end` mean exactly
the same thing — membership in one named boundary. Co-location in the source
file is a convenience, not the contract. The contract is the name.

## A reusable profile, not an `is:` keyword

Caps and limits are options on the sandbox: `grant:` lists the capabilities
the Dock may fulfil, `memory:` caps the footprint, `timeout:` bounds a call.
When several sandboxes want the same posture, you do **not** invent a keyword
for it — there is no `is:` here. You write a plain Elixir module attribute
and pass it as options. A sandbox profile is just data:

@pricing grant: [:net, :kv], memory: "128mb", timeout: 30_000

sandbox :pricing, @pricing do
  # :tax here, and :score joins by name
end

That `@pricing` attribute is an ordinary keyword list. Passing it to
`sandbox :pricing, @pricing do … end` applies all three limits at once, and
the same attribute can configure a second sandbox with identical posture. No
DSL, no special form — a value the language already understands, reused the
way you reuse any value. This is the [[Lead]] principle showing up in
configuration: structure over syntax.

## How the caps are actually enforced

Granting `[:net, :kv]` is not paperwork; it is enforced, and *where* depends
on placement:

- For **wasm units** (a foreign-language unit on the server, or anything on
  the client), the grant is enforced **by construction**. The generated WIT
  contract — the thing you never write or read — only *imports* the host
  functions the grant allowed. A unit with no `:net` grant has no network
  import in its component at all. It cannot call out because the capability
  was never linked. This is the WIT-import-by-construction guarantee.
- For **native server units** like `:tax` and [[score]], there is no wasm
  wall, so the **nexus enforces the boundary at the Dock**. Every capability
  request crosses the Dock membrane, where the nexus checks it against the
  sandbox's grant before a provider fulfils it. Same grant, same denial of
  un-granted calls — the wasm version proves it statically, the native
  version checks it at the seam.

Either way the rule is identical: a unit may use a capability only if its
sandbox granted it. `:tax` does pure arithmetic, so it never exercises
`:net` or `:kv` at all; the grant is the *ceiling*, not a requirement.

## Crossing a sandbox boundary

A quote in a foreign currency needs an exchange rate, and rates come from the
`:fx` unit. But `:fx` does not live in `:pricing` — it lives in its own
sandbox, `:rates`, because reaching the rates API is a network capability we
want walled off separately from the pricing math:

@rates grant: [:net], memory: "64mb", timeout: 10_000

sandbox :rates, @rates do
  server compute :fx do
    def rate(from, to) do
      # reaches the rates provider; allowed because :rates granted :net
      {:ok, fetch_rate("api.rates.com", from, to)}
    end
  end
end

Now `:tax` (in `:pricing`) wants to convert a gross figure using `:fx` (in
`:rates`). That call **crosses a sandbox boundary**, and two rules govern it:

1. **Cross-sandbox calls are host-brokered.** `:tax` does not reach into
   `:rates` directly. The call goes through the Dock, which brokers the
   handoff between the two boundaries — the same membrane that fulfils a
   capability also mediates a unit-to-unit call across a wall.
2. **Capabilities flow by grant, not by inheritance.** `:tax` calling into
   `:fx` does **not** lend `:tax` the `:net` grant that `:rates` holds. The
   network capability stays on the `:rates` side of the wall; `:tax` only
   receives the *result* of the brokered call. A boundary you call across is
   not a boundary you inherit through.

Here is the cross-sandbox edge in `:tax`, converting a gross amount:

server compute :tax, in: :pricing do
  def quote_in(amount, region, currency) do
    base = quote(amount, region)

    case fx_rate(:usd, currency) do
      {:ok, rate} ->
        converted = round(base.gross * rate)
        Map.merge(base, %{currency: currency, gross_local: converted})

      {:error, _reason} ->
        # rates unreachable: quote stays in USD, no silent guess
        Map.put(base, :currency, :usd)
    end
  end

  # the brokered hop into sandbox :rates — :tax never gains :net by it
  defp fx_rate(from, to), do: rate(from, to)
end

`:tax` asks for a rate and gets back `{:ok, rate}` or `{:error, reason}` — a
tagged-tuple variant, the [[Lead]] way of saying *this can fail*. It never
holds a network handle of its own. If the broker returns an error because
`:rates` could not reach `api.rates.com`, `:tax` falls back to a USD quote
rather than inventing a number. The wall held; the grant did not leak; the
failure was explicit.

## Why this matters for the workbook

The weave gate reads these sandboxes as part of the [[code graph]]: each
`sandbox` is a node, each grant is an audited capability edge, each
cross-sandbox call is a brokered edge it can trace. `work why :tax` will show
that `:tax` reaches `:fx` *across* `:rates`, and that the `:net` capability
lives on the `:rates` node and was never granted to `:pricing`. The money
path is grouped, capped, and legible — and we wrote zero WIT and zero JSON to
get there, only Elixir modules and a couple of reusable attributes.

Next, the [[forecast]] and [[risk]] chapters take sandboxes polyglot: a
Rust+Zig numeric kernel and a Python risk unit, each a wasm component behind
the very same grant-by-construction wall described here.
